sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch13-21> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Embryological facts follow from late-appearing variations inherited at corresponding ages" ;
    dct:description "The leading facts of embryology are explained because, in the descendants of a common progenitor, successive slight modifications generally do not appear at a very early period of life (though they may be caused earlier) and are inherited at a corresponding later age, leaving embryos little modified." ;
    delphi:context "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XIII, “Mutual Affinities of Organic Beings: Morphology: Embryology: Rudimentary Organs”; section “EMBRYOLOGY, laws of, explained by variations not supervening at an early age, and being inherited at a corresponding age”. Scope: animals generally" ;
    dct:type "mechanism" .
}

